# std/low_io.hd -- the floor of Haard's input and output, and nothing else.
#
# Eight functions whose body is 'pass'. Nothing in Haard can reach a C
# library, so the emitter writes those eight bodies -- by name, and only
# inside this module, which is record 0030. It is meant to be deleted: when a
# real foreign interface is decided, these become ordinary declarations of it
# and nothing above them changes.
#
# **Nobody imports this to print something.** 'std.io' next door has 'print'
# and 'println', and 'std.file' has the File they are written on top of; both
# are ordinary Haard, which is the point of stopping here. The name says what
# it is: the low half, one character at a time.
#
# C's stdio and not C++'s streams, and the reason is the handle. A 'FILE*' is
# an opaque pointer and is an 'i8*' here with nothing lost; an 'std::ofstream'
# is an object, and Haard cannot name its type, know its size, or run its
# constructor. And 'stdout' is a 'FILE*' too -- so one set of primitives covers
# the console and a file alike, which is what lets a File written in Haard
# print to the terminal.

# null when the file will not open
def __io_open_read : i8*
    @path : char*
    pass

def __io_open_write : i8*
    @path : char*
    pass

# The character. What it gives back once the file has ended is nothing anyone
# should read, and __io_at_end below is how that is asked.
#
# C's own fgetc answers both at once by giving back an int and -1 for the end,
# and that is exactly what record 0018 will not have: an i32 does not compare
# to a 'char' literal, so 'if c == \'\\n\':' would not even be writable. The
# rule pushed the design somewhere better -- the value and the end are two
# questions, and each has its own type
def __io_read : char
    @handle : i8*
    pass

# whether a read has run past the end. Asked AFTER a read, the way C's feof is
def __io_at_end : bool
    @handle : i8*
    pass

def __io_write : void
    @handle : i8*
    @c : char
    pass

def __io_close : void
    @handle : i8*
    pass

def __io_stdout : i8*
    pass

def __io_stdin : i8*
    pass

def __io_stderr : i8*
    pass
